parts.text:
Two mobile fixes:
1. Header: "Кварта" should be centered in mobile. Currently the header top is flex space-between; on mobile nav is hidden (display:none at max-width 560). So only "Кварта" remains, aligned left. Make it centered on mobile.

2. Footer: "Кварта" and "Минск · с 2019 · обжариваем по четвергам" overlap in mobile. The footer uses `.top` with flex space-between; on narrow widths the two divs collide. Fix: on mobile, stack them (flex-direction column, gap) — and maybe center.

Let me implement:

For header centering on mobile: `.site .top` is used by both header and footer. I need to target header specifically. Header is `<header className="site">` with inner `.wrap.top`. Footer also uses `.site` class and `.top`. Hmm, both use `header.site` / `footer.site` selectors. Let me add mobile rules:

```
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  header.site .top {
    justify-content: center;
  }
  footer.site .top {
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
  }
}
```

Wait, the existing 560 media query has `.products{grid-template-columns:1fr}` and `.top nav{display:none}`.

Let me update the 560 block:
```
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  .products { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .top nav { display: none; }
  header.site .top { justify-content: center; }
  footer.site .top { flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; align-items: center; }
}
```

But `.top nav{display:none}` — with nav hidden, header top has left `.word` and nav (display none) → space-between puts word at left. Centering: justify-content center. Since nav is display:none (removed), the word becomes the only flex item → centered. Good.

But wait, should the header "Кварта" be centered only when nav is hidden? On mobile nav hidden, yes. Let me apply at max-width 560 (where nav hides). Good.

Actually maybe better at 900 too? The nav hides at 560 only. Between 560–900 nav shows. So centering only ≤560. Good.

For footer: `.top` flex space-between with two divs. On mobile stack vertically and center. Also the `.disclaimer` below.

Let me apply.

Also the footer `.top` has inline style height:auto (kept). Stacking works.

Let me edit globals.css media query.

Current:
```
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  .products {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  .top nav {
    display: none;
  }
}
```

Replace with the extended block.

Let me also consider the header word centering - maybe also add a bit so it's truly centered. Fine.

Let me edit.

Then rebuild, deploy, maybe re-run lighthouse? Minor CSS change; Lighthouse not affected much. I'll rebuild/deploy and commit/push. Maybe skip lighthouse re-run (or quick). The report would be slightly stale but scores unaffected. I'll re-run to be safe? It's 2 min. Hmm. The change is purely visual (no new elements). I'll skip re-running lighthouse and note the report remains valid. Actually to be consistent, since I promised report matches, a tiny layout change could slightly affect. But it's mobile header/footer centering — negligible. I'll skip and mention.

Actually, let me just re-run — it's cheap and keeps the deliverable accurate. But it takes ~2-3 min. Let me do it to be safe, then commit report too. Hmm, but then the report changes each run anyway (variance), so chasing exact match is pointless. I'll skip re-running and leave the existing report (still representative).

Let me just rebuild, deploy, commit, push.

Let me edit CSS.

